  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Becoming blustery and cold tonight into Friday \nwith mountain flurries and snow showers.\n\nAn upper-level low will track over the Tennessee Valley this \nevening before moving into the southern Ohio Valley by Friday \nmorning. A coastal disturbance spawned by this system will \ncontinue northward along the I-95 corridor into tonight, \nbringing an end to the 1 to 3 inches of rain and strong \nnortheast gusts as it exits the region. Once that first phase \ndeparts, the upper-level low itself will push a second round of \nprecipitation and gusty west-southwest winds mainly into the \nmountains overnight. Below 3,500 feet, this moisture will start \nout as a rain and snow mix, while higher elevations will see \nmostly light snow. As colder air filters in throughout the day \non Friday, all lingering precipitation will transition into \nlight snow or flurries. Warm ground temperatures at lower \nelevations will prevent snow from accumulating on road surfaces,\nthough the highest peaks will pick up an inch or two. While \nFriday's winds will not match the intensity of earlier gusts, \nthey will remain strong enough to require securing loose outdoor\nitems. Blustery conditions and below-normal temperatures will \nkeep wind chills in the 20s across the mountains and the 30s \nacross the foothills and Piedmont on Friday, before warmer, \ndrier weather finally returns for the weekend.",
